}]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[1987,1,1]]},"ISBN":["9789027235480","9789027279064"],"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1075\/cilt.54.08ton","archive":["Portico"],"ISSN":["0304-0763"],"issn-type":[{"type":"print","value":"0304-0763"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[1987,1,1]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,3]],"date-time":"2026-09-03T21:50:06Z","timestamp":1788472206443,"version":"build-2803163510"},"reference-count":64,"publisher":"John Benjamins Publishing Company","issue":"1","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["LTBA"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2019,6,14]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title><jats:p>This paper surveys the forms of dual and plural pronouns across the Tibeto-Burman languages, and offers a reconstruction of the non-singular pronouns, and a general account of how various branches and languages have diverged from this original system. We can certainly reconstruct two, perhaps three, person-number portmanteaus: #<jats:italic>i<\/jats:italic>1<jats:sc>pl<\/jats:sc>, or perhaps 1<jats:sc>pl.inc<\/jats:sc>, #<jats:italic>ni<\/jats:italic>2<jats:sc>pl<\/jats:sc>, and, less certainly, #<jats:italic>ka<\/jats:italic>1<jats:sc>pl.exc<\/jats:sc>. We also reconstruct #<jats:italic>tsi<\/jats:italic><jats:sc>dual<\/jats:sc>which combined with singular pronouns to make dual forms. This construction was the model on which most daughter languages have innovated an analytic system of person and number marking, with distinct person and dual and\/or plural morphemes combining to make the morphologically complex but semantically transparent compositional forms found in the majority of languages.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1075\/ltba.18006.del","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2019,6,14]],"date-time":"2019-06-14T11:06:57Z","timestamp":1560510417000},"page":"110-136","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":3,"title":["Non-singular pronouns in Tibeto-Burman (Trans-Himalayan)"],"prefix":"10.1075","volume":"42","author":[{"given":"Scott","family":"DeLancey","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Oregon"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"1757","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2019,6,14]]},"reference":[{"key":"CIT0001","volume-title":"Sketch of Thulung Grammar","author":"Allen","year":"1975"},{"key":"CIT0002","first-page":"418","article-title":"Tshangla","volume-title":"The Sino-Tibetan languages","author":"Andvik","year":"2017","edition":"2"},{"key":"CIT0003","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.31979\/etd.v36r-dk3u"},{"key":"CIT0004","volume-title":"Pronouns and pronominal morphology in Tibeto-Burman","author":"Bauman","year":"1975"},{"issue":"2","key":"CIT0005","first-page":"1","article-title":"The first person pronoun in Archaic Chinese, with a note on duplex characters","volume":"21","author":"Benedict","year":"1998","journal-title":"Linguistics of the Tibeto-Burman Area"},{"key":"CIT0006","volume-title":"A Grammar of Sunuwar","author":"Borchers","year":"2008"},{"key":"CIT0007","volume-title":"Chang morphology","author":"Chang","year":"2013"},{"key":"CIT0008","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1515\/9783110198522"},{"key":"CIT0009","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1163\/19606028-00442p02"},{"key":"CIT0010","unstructured":"DeLancey , Scott . Under review . First person pronominals in Kuki-Naga languages ."},{"key":"CIT0011","volume-title":"Tangkhul grammar (A descriptive model)","author":"Devi","year":"2014"},{"key":"CIT0012","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1163\/9789004279773"},{"key":"CIT0013","volume-title":"A functional grammar of Khatso","author":"Donlay","year":"2015"},{"key":"CIT0014","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1017\/S0041977X00005528"},{"key":"CIT0015","volume-title":"The Mru language: A preliminary grammatical sketch","author":"Ebersole","year":"1996"},{"key":"CIT0016","volume-title":"Camling (Chamling)","author":"Ebert","year":"1997"},{"ke